BlitzMax.Compiler is the reusable staged compiler pipeline used by bcc2.

Most consumers begin with #TBlitzMaxCompiler. #Compile accepts source text and a
dependency resolver; #CompileFile loads a root source file and uses the standard
filesystem resolver. Both return a #TCompilerResult containing language analysis,
the generic application plan, typed compiler IR, timing information, and diagnostics.

Compilation and output emission are deliberately separate. After checking
#TCompilerResult.Succeeded, a build host may emit runtime-compatible C, a runtime
header, a compact module interface, generic template artifacts, and a generic
manifest. #PlanBuildOutputs combines those products into the file plan consumed by
a build driver.

```blitzmax
Local options:TCompilerOptions = TCompilerOptions.CreateDefault()
Local result:TCompilerResult = TBlitzMaxCompiler.CompileFile("program.bmx", options)

If result.Succeeded() Then
	Local diagnostics:TCompilerDiagnostic[]
	Local cSource:String = TBlitzMaxCompiler.EmitRuntimeC(result, diagnostics)
End If
```

#TCompilerOptions controls the target, build mode, conditional symbols,
instrumentation, application identity, and other compilation details. Call
#RefreshConditionalSymbols after changing target-related fields directly.

Consumers interested only in parsing or semantic analysis should import
#BlitzMax.Language instead; it has no dependency on compiler lowering or a backend.
